---
title: "The Story of the Name Celise"
subtitle: "An Interactive Exploration of U.S. Baby Names"
format:
  html:
    theme: flatly
    toc: true
execute:
  echo: false
---

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| message: false

library(tidyverse)
library(plotly)
library(DT)

# Find all yearly data files
files <- list.files("data", pattern = "yob\\d+\\.txt", full.names = TRUE)

# Read all files into one table and extract the year from the filename
baby_names <- read_csv(
  files,
  id = "file",
  col_names = c("name", "sex", "count"),
  show_col_types = FALSE
) |>
  mutate(year = parse_number(file)) |>
  select(-file)
```

## Trend of "Celise" (Past 100 Years)

```{r}
#| label: celise-trend

celise_100yr <- baby_names |>
  filter(name == "Celise", year >= 1926) |>
  group_by(year) |>
  summarize(total = sum(count), .groups = "drop")

p <- ggplot(celise_100yr, aes(x = year, y = total, text = paste0("Year: ", year, "<br>Babies: ", total))) +
  geom_area(fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.2) +
  geom_line(color = "steelblue", linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_point(color = "steelblue", size = 2) +
  labs(
    title = "Popularity of the Name Celise (1926–2025)",
    x = "Year",
    y = "Number of Babies"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

ggplotly(p, tooltip = "text") |>
  rangeslider()
```

The name Celise first appeared in SSA records in 1962 and has remained rare, with between 5 and 11 babies recorded per year. Use the range slider under the chart to zoom into specific decades!

## Female vs. Male Breakdown

```{r}
#| label: celise-sex

celise_sex <- baby_names |>
  filter(name == "Celise") |>
  group_by(sex) |>
  summarize(total = sum(count), .groups = "drop") |>
  complete(sex = c("F", "M"), fill = list(total = 0)) |>
  mutate(
    sex_label = if_else(sex == "F", "Female", "Male"),
    percentage = total / sum(total) * 100
  )

p_sex <- ggplot(celise_sex, aes(x = sex_label, y = percentage, fill = sex_label)) +
  geom_col(width = 0.4, show.legend = FALSE) +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, 100), labels = \(x) paste0(x, "%")) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Female" = "#e41a1c", "Male" = "#377eb8")) +
  labs(
    title = "Percentage of Babies Named Celise by Sex",
    x = "Sex",
    y = "Percentage"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

ggplotly(p_sex)
```

Across all years in the Social Security dataset, **100%** of babies named Celise (207 total) are recorded as female, with 0 recorded as male.

## Historical Records Explorer

```{r}
#| label: celise-table

celise_records <- baby_names |>
  filter(name == "Celise") |>
  arrange(desc(year)) |>
  select(Year = year, Sex = sex, Count = count)

datatable(
  celise_records,
  options = list(pageLength = 10, dom = "ftp"),
  rownames = FALSE
)
```

